#====================================================================
# E345 draft - Cross-built provider metadata relocation must cover suffixes and slash styles
#====================================================================

id:                     E345
trigger_tool:           qmake|Conan|pkg-config|CMakeDeps
phase:                  package-consume
error_signature:        module .*\.pri.*not found|No such file or directory.*conan-home|cannot find.*\.prl|stale.*\.conan2/p/b

severity:               3
category:               ohos, conan, cross-compile, qmake, metadata, relocation
match_keywords:         package_folder, package metadata, .pri, .prl, .la, .pc, .cmake, Conan build root, host path, slash normalization, cross provider
title:                  Cross-built Conan provider metadata must be relocated by suffix and slash style before target consumers run

root_cause:             A cross-built provider can embed its temporary Conan build or package root in generated metadata. The consumer then follows a host-only path even though the provider files were transferred correctly. Matching only Unix paths or only one metadata suffix leaves another qmake, libtool, pkg-config, or CMake entry stale. In the recipe environment, Environment.append on LD_LIBRARY_PATH replaces the worker's SDK runtime path; use append_path so linker tools retain required host libraries such as libxml2. CMakeDeps package and component config names must be taken from its generated guidance; provider component files are not necessarily on the consumer's CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH.

affected_scope:         Any cross-compiled Conan dependency whose generated .pri/.prl/.la/.pc/.cmake files retain a build or package root, especially when a Windows host creates metadata later consumed by an OHOS target.

verification_cmd:       Inspect every metadata file under the provider package; replace only identity-bound old roots with the current package_folder; assert no old root remains; run a real target consumer that loads the provider API and record target architecture.

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           metadata-root-relocation-and-consumer-smoke
rec_description:        Derive the provider package_folder from Conan dependency metadata. Scan all relevant suffixes and both POSIX and Windows path spellings. Rewrite only exact provider build/package roots, preserve unrelated text, and use Environment.append_path for LD_LIBRARY_PATH overlays so SDK linker runtimes remain visible. Read the CMakeDeps generated find_package guidance and consume those package-level names/targets rather than guessing component config paths. Bind test() to the exact executable emitted by add_executable; a successful build with a stale test path is not a passing consumer. Then run the installed consumer and verify its output plus ELF machine type. A qmake configure success alone is insufficient.
rec_command:            target consumer build and run; provider metadata stale-root scan
rec_auto_fix:           false
rec_impl:               conan-recipe

fallback_priority:      2
fallback_approach:      fail-closed-metadata-scan
fallback_description:   If the provider metadata schema is unknown, do not guess a replacement. Mark the package non-consumable until the exact root and generated-file contract are identified.
fallback_command:
fallback_auto_fix:      false
fallback_impl:          conan-recipe

forbid_1_pattern:       scan only .pri or only Unix slash paths
forbid_1_reason:        A different generated suffix or Windows path can still expose the stale host root.
forbid_1_consequence:   Target qmake/CMake/pkg-config resolution fails after transfer, or links against an unintended host path.
forbid_2_pattern:       claim package build success without a real installed-provider consumer
forbid_2_reason:        Build completion does not exercise metadata resolution or runtime loading.
forbid_2_consequence:   A broken package can pass compile-only evidence and fail on the target.

principles:             P013,P014
causes:                 []
caused_by:              []
similar_to:             []
conflicts_with:         []
prerequisite_of:        []

